1
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:11,327
[SPEAKER_06]: We drove up to Pittsburgh on Friday afternoon, leaving here about 1.30 and got into our motel about 5 in Brentwood.

2
00:00:13,748 --> 00:00:19,012
[SPEAKER_06]: And we relaxed a while and finally went out for supper at Arby's and then over to Knetzer's.

3
00:00:19,532 --> 00:00:23,415
[SPEAKER_06]: Got there about, just about on time, quarter to 8 or so in the evening.

4
00:00:24,716 --> 00:00:27,017
[SPEAKER_06]: And left about quarter to 12.

5
00:00:28,619 --> 00:00:30,640
[SPEAKER_06]: They were real nice to us, the Robinsons.

6
00:00:31,673 --> 00:00:35,736
[SPEAKER_06]: Mr. Knutzer was just as interested as he could be.

7
00:00:36,696 --> 00:00:40,779
[SPEAKER_06]: They thought, they tried to get him to go to bed a couple of times, but he kept not going to bed.

8
00:00:41,319 --> 00:00:46,282
[SPEAKER_06]: He went upstairs once, they thought he was going to bed, but he grabbed his pipe and came back down and puffed his pipe.

9
00:00:48,624 --> 00:00:52,026
[SPEAKER_06]: While I was taking pictures, or trying to take pictures with the 35mm camera.

10
00:00:52,066 --> 00:00:52,186
[SPEAKER_06]: Well,

11
00:00:59,885 --> 00:01:09,731
[SPEAKER_06]: Saturday morning we got up about 7.30 and had a leisurely breakfast and went looking for the bookstore, which was down the road a little bit, but it was not open until noon.

12
00:01:10,592 --> 00:01:13,754
[SPEAKER_06]: It was raining and foggy on Saturday morning, yesterday morning.

13
00:01:15,175 --> 00:01:17,456
[SPEAKER_06]: We're back home now, Sunday morning.

14
00:01:17,556 --> 00:01:22,439
[SPEAKER_06]: I'm filling up this side of the tape.

15
00:01:25,341 --> 00:01:28,023
[SPEAKER_06]: We decided to go back to

16
00:01:29,987 --> 00:01:38,131
[SPEAKER_06]: Robinsons because there's a couple more pictures I thought I ought to get since we were up there now, get the main ones.

17
00:01:38,932 --> 00:01:43,094
[SPEAKER_06]: There's Scrapbook Two I thought might be nice, a lot of good stories in there.

18
00:01:44,134 --> 00:01:48,977
[SPEAKER_06]: And they were on size pages which could be easily xeroxed.

19
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:56,355
[SPEAKER_06]: So we stopped there about 9.30 or so at the Robinson's, but no sign of life.

20
00:01:56,375 --> 00:02:17,553
[SPEAKER_06]: I didn't want to go waking everybody up, so we just forgot about it, went on over to Boone, which is where we had the appointment scheduled, and got there a little early, but sat in the car for a few minutes, and about quarter to ten we went on in.

21
00:02:19,578 --> 00:02:22,761
[SPEAKER_06]: And they were ready for us, and we had a nice interview at Boone's.

22
00:02:24,242 --> 00:02:27,404
[SPEAKER_06]: And I got, well, we left there about a quarter after 12.

23
00:02:27,625 --> 00:02:45,199
[SPEAKER_06]: I taped about an hour and a half, and I showed him the pictures I had copied, returned those that I'd borrowed, and promised to make prints of any of those that he would like to have copies of for his children, or for kids sending him for autographs to give him some extra copies.

24
00:02:45,219 --> 00:02:46,660
[SPEAKER_06]: So he's going to look over the list,

25
00:02:48,183 --> 00:02:53,747
[SPEAKER_06]: see what he has there, what I have that he would like to have.

26
00:02:57,010 --> 00:03:03,855
[SPEAKER_06]: Then I took about 20 or 25 pictures with the 35mm camera, about 20, I guess.

27
00:03:05,377 --> 00:03:09,320
[SPEAKER_06]: We'd taken about 10 over at Knetzler's, and we took about 20 more.

28
00:03:10,621 --> 00:03:14,364
[SPEAKER_06]: I think I had 32 taken by the time we left the place.

29
00:03:16,442 --> 00:03:18,323
[SPEAKER_06]: from the scrapbook and a few odd ones.

30
00:03:19,103 --> 00:03:22,265
[SPEAKER_06]: And he gave me a pile of pictures to copy, some huge ones.

31
00:03:24,526 --> 00:03:30,629
[SPEAKER_06]: And they wouldn't even fit in that big cardboard container I had for the big pictures.

32
00:03:32,430 --> 00:03:36,652
[SPEAKER_06]: So I had them all scattered about here in the house now, trying to get copies made.

33
00:03:40,188 --> 00:03:49,731
[SPEAKER_06]: We got instructions from Luke Boone how to get over to Aetna on the north side of Pittsburgh to interview Ralph Michaels with whom we had an interview scheduled at about 2 o'clock, 2.30.

34
00:03:50,772 --> 00:03:53,473
[SPEAKER_06]: He gave us very good instructions and we went back to the motel.

35
00:03:54,533 --> 00:04:04,156
[SPEAKER_06]: Got there about 1, changed clothes because we didn't want to be, we wanted to be comfortably attired for the afternoon and then the drive back home.

36
00:04:06,877 --> 00:04:08,538
[SPEAKER_06]: So we changed clothes and

37
00:04:09,892 --> 00:04:34,605
[SPEAKER_06]: loaded the car up and got away from the Brentwood Motor Lodge in Brentwood there about 1.30 and drove back much of the way we came into Brentwood, crossed over the tri-river bridge or something a little bit west of the center of Pittsburgh,

38
00:04:35,710 --> 00:04:39,171
[SPEAKER_06]: You could look across and see three river stadiums we crossed over.

39
00:04:39,191 --> 00:04:41,231
[SPEAKER_06]: And this put us on the north side of the Ohio.

40
00:04:42,452 --> 00:04:56,975
[SPEAKER_06]: And then we wound our way along Ohio Street and worked our way out to Hettner, which we reached about, oh, 10 after 2, a quarter after 2.

41
00:04:58,155 --> 00:05:00,916
[SPEAKER_06]: Took us about 45 minutes from the motel.

42
00:05:01,876 --> 00:05:03,096
[SPEAKER_06]: There wasn't too much traffic.

43
00:05:03,136 --> 00:05:04,177
[SPEAKER_06]: We avoided the center of town.

44
00:05:07,335 --> 00:05:09,197
[SPEAKER_06]: The weather had cleared a little bit.

45
00:05:09,217 --> 00:05:10,978
[SPEAKER_06]: It wasn't raining and foggy anymore.

46
00:05:10,998 --> 00:05:12,519
[SPEAKER_06]: It came patches of sunshine.

47
00:05:12,539 --> 00:05:19,103
[SPEAKER_06]: We were listening to Buzsorgsky's Boris Gudunov on the way, by the way, in Metropolitan Opera.

48
00:05:22,605 --> 00:05:29,529
[SPEAKER_06]: Well, we got into Etna, and it was a tangled of narrow, hilly, windy, cobblestone streets.

49
00:05:29,929 --> 00:05:32,010
[SPEAKER_06]: We couldn't quite find out where we were going.

50
00:05:32,030 --> 00:05:34,752
[SPEAKER_06]: We got in wrong way streets going down steep hills.

51
00:05:36,563 --> 00:05:41,646
[SPEAKER_06]: one-way streets going, up steep hills, it all twisted around.

52
00:05:41,666 --> 00:05:50,310
[SPEAKER_06]: We finally found the place, Michael's house at 123 Locust Street, right across from the Elks Club.

53
00:05:52,231 --> 00:05:59,275
[SPEAKER_06]: Michael had told me that if, to park right in front of the house there, and then to come over and see him in the Elks Club if he wasn't home.

54
00:06:00,655 --> 00:06:01,916
[SPEAKER_06]: And so that's what I did.

55
00:06:01,956 --> 00:06:05,778
[SPEAKER_06]: He went to the house, nobody there, and then went over to the Elks Club,

56
00:06:06,288 --> 00:06:17,215
[SPEAKER_06]: After I got downstairs and got caught up in a teenage bowling congress, I went upstairs and pressed a button and waited for about ten minutes.

57
00:06:17,235 --> 00:06:18,796
[SPEAKER_06]: Finally, a woman came and let me in.

58
00:06:19,837 --> 00:06:30,884
[SPEAKER_06]: And then I went to the check, or wherever you check your coats at, that counter.

59
00:06:32,325 --> 00:06:35,787
[SPEAKER_06]: And the guy said, yeah, he was in at the bar drinking beer.

60
00:06:36,414 --> 00:06:40,378
[SPEAKER_06]: So he'd take me in there, so he took a few more minutes, finally I got in there.

61
00:06:41,759 --> 00:06:46,423
[SPEAKER_06]: Michael was at the bar with a lot of other people watching the basketball game on television.

62
00:06:49,125 --> 00:06:52,808
[SPEAKER_06]: And right away he told me he wasn't going to be interviewed.

63
00:06:53,389 --> 00:06:54,650
[SPEAKER_06]: It was a big disappointment.

64
00:06:54,870 --> 00:06:58,794
[SPEAKER_06]: He told me to come on up and to see him, and yeah.

65
00:07:00,433 --> 00:07:01,253
[SPEAKER_06]: He wouldn't mind.

66
00:07:01,874 --> 00:07:03,235
[SPEAKER_06]: At least that was the impression I got.

67
00:07:03,255 --> 00:07:18,486
[SPEAKER_06]: There were one or two things he said when I talked to him on the phone last Monday that indicated he might not want to talk about this or that, but I at no time got the feeling he was going to clam up completely.

68
00:07:18,686 --> 00:07:26,571
[SPEAKER_06]: But no sooner had he walked over from the bar, behind the bar, that he talked to me than I got right over.

69
00:07:26,592 --> 00:07:28,133
[SPEAKER_06]: He was pleasant enough, but

70
00:07:29,370 --> 00:07:30,711
[SPEAKER_06]: He wasn't going to be interviewed.

71
00:07:30,831 --> 00:07:34,494
[SPEAKER_06]: No way would he be interviewed, especially on tape.

72
00:07:36,976 --> 00:07:47,384
[SPEAKER_06]: Well, which took me by surprise completely, and for a moment it looked as if he was just going to go right back to the bar and dismiss me on the spot, and that would be the end of it.

73
00:07:49,446 --> 00:07:51,628
[SPEAKER_06]: He was pleasant enough in all of this,

74
00:07:52,935 --> 00:07:54,736
[SPEAKER_06]: but very firm.

75
00:07:54,776 --> 00:07:57,137
[SPEAKER_06]: He kept telling me he's a police chief for 17 years.

76
00:07:58,578 --> 00:08:00,759
[SPEAKER_06]: This was old stuff way back there.

77
00:08:00,779 --> 00:08:02,680
[SPEAKER_06]: He didn't want to remember it or bring it back.

78
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:06,621
[SPEAKER_06]: I never could quite pin it down what the reason was.

79
00:08:07,642 --> 00:08:12,284
[SPEAKER_06]: He said he'd called Luke Boone earlier in the week and talked to him, checking up on me.

80
00:08:13,104 --> 00:08:16,466
[SPEAKER_06]: He thought a lot about this, and he just decided he didn't want to be interviewed.

81
00:08:16,506 --> 00:08:20,828
[SPEAKER_06]: He'd lost his wife and his sister a couple years ago, three losses right in a row.

82
00:08:21,740 --> 00:08:24,761
[SPEAKER_06]: His life is kind of up in the air, I gather.

83
00:08:25,161 --> 00:08:27,241
[SPEAKER_06]: He had a good reputation to maintain.

84
00:08:27,961 --> 00:08:29,541
[SPEAKER_06]: He had a good reputation as a policeman.

85
00:08:29,581 --> 00:08:30,982
[SPEAKER_06]: He didn't want anything to happen.

86
00:08:31,022 --> 00:08:37,343
[SPEAKER_06]: He seemed to feel that by being interviewed on tape there was going to be some damage to his reputation, his career.

87
00:08:39,183 --> 00:08:49,985
[SPEAKER_06]: It was just difficult to nail down specifically what his thoughts were, other than just a general distrust of tape recorders, which you get from time to time.

88
00:08:51,285 --> 00:08:58,109
[SPEAKER_06]: He said he'd been in trouble about this upcoming visit of mine all week, and he just decided he wasn't going to be in it.

89
00:08:58,909 --> 00:09:13,336
[SPEAKER_06]: Well, he did loosen up a little bit and talk to me behind, next to the bar there, and he became quite expansive and confidential, and after each story he'd tell, he'd say, this was a great story, this should be on tape, and then he'd back off again while he was going back to the bar and dismiss me again.

90
00:09:13,997 --> 00:09:20,560
[SPEAKER_06]: Every time I seemed to indicate that these were great stories and should be preserved for posterity while he was

91
00:09:21,255 --> 00:09:28,501
[SPEAKER_06]: right away on his toes about to, right away on the brink of cutting me off.

92
00:09:31,783 --> 00:09:37,408
[SPEAKER_06]: We chatted there about 20-25 minutes, I suppose, telling stories and good stories.

93
00:09:38,148 --> 00:09:43,212
[SPEAKER_06]: And I was listening and gaining his confidence as we went along, I could see that.

94
00:09:43,592 --> 00:09:46,955
[SPEAKER_06]: Then he got his wallet out and showed me pictures of himself as a police chief.

95
00:09:48,671 --> 00:09:55,573
[SPEAKER_06]: Pictures of his wife who was in a bathing suit back in the vintage 1920s, 30s.

96
00:09:55,613 --> 00:09:57,593
[SPEAKER_06]: She was quite a beautiful gal.

97
00:09:59,113 --> 00:10:06,435
[SPEAKER_06]: I guess her loss was very, well, it's still bothering him.

98
00:10:06,555 --> 00:10:07,835
[SPEAKER_06]: He had no children.

99
00:10:12,357 --> 00:10:17,898
[SPEAKER_06]: At the end of the interview, he kept saying what a nice guy I was.

100
00:10:19,514 --> 00:10:25,058
[SPEAKER_06]: much he appreciated my understanding, his not wanting to be interviewed.

101
00:10:26,639 --> 00:10:32,102
[SPEAKER_06]: He thought what I was doing was great, wished me a lot of luck, but he just couldn't contribute himself.

102
00:10:33,383 --> 00:10:39,346
[SPEAKER_06]: And he said I was a regular guy, and he was getting more chummy all the way along in that respect.

103
00:10:40,707 --> 00:10:48,432
[SPEAKER_06]: And maybe if I had stayed with him longer and gotten Reedy in out of the car to help loosen him up a little bit, he might have been able to get something out of him.

104
00:10:49,556 --> 00:11:02,200
[SPEAKER_06]: He was big and tough and outgoing and tough in a sense that if you pushed him, he'd react rather strongly.

105
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:05,701
[SPEAKER_06]: But he was a hale fellow, well-met chap.

106
00:11:05,761 --> 00:11:11,702
[SPEAKER_06]: He knew everybody and talked loud and confidently and self-assuredly.

107
00:11:11,722 --> 00:11:16,744
[SPEAKER_06]: He reminded me of a John Burnworth manner with an Ed Sherman look.

108
00:11:18,780 --> 00:11:21,682
[SPEAKER_06]: He looks a lot like Ed Sherman, the Muskingum athletic director.

109
00:11:21,742 --> 00:11:25,665
[SPEAKER_06]: His physical appearance is big, about six foot probably.

110
00:11:26,686 --> 00:11:30,109
[SPEAKER_06]: And he's probably doesn't weigh anymore now.

111
00:11:30,149 --> 00:11:36,394
[SPEAKER_06]: He's in his playing ball, 185 pounds, good build, no stomach, good features.

112
00:11:36,454 --> 00:11:40,157
[SPEAKER_06]: He's got his hair, light gray hair, or dark gray hair.

113
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:45,941
[SPEAKER_06]: Well-lined but well-shaped face.

114
00:11:48,318 --> 00:11:52,200
[SPEAKER_06]: dark sport of brown slacks and a brown jacket.

115
00:11:52,220 --> 00:12:01,265
[SPEAKER_06]: I had one of those brown t-shirts, not a t-shirt, a dress t-shirt, perhaps, underneath, maybe a turtleneck, I've forgotten now.

116
00:12:03,126 --> 00:12:12,832
[SPEAKER_06]: I mean, he looked in good shape and he was very jovial and so on, but I wasn't going to get an interview, a really detailed interview.

117
00:12:14,953 --> 00:12:16,894
[SPEAKER_06]: Well, here's some of the few things I did get.

118
00:12:21,035 --> 00:12:22,396
[SPEAKER_06]: Let me light my cigar again.

119
00:12:22,416 --> 00:12:25,399
[SPEAKER_06]: It's already lit.

120
00:12:31,504 --> 00:12:39,451
[SPEAKER_06]: He came up in 1921, and I didn't really, I should have, but I didn't get his outline of his career.

121
00:12:41,093 --> 00:12:44,496
[SPEAKER_06]: He came up in 21, or he came into baseball.

122
00:12:52,428 --> 00:12:53,729
[SPEAKER_06]: I don't know who he was with.

123
00:12:53,789 --> 00:12:55,709
[SPEAKER_06]: Apparently he was pirate property, however.

124
00:12:57,070 --> 00:12:59,650
[SPEAKER_06]: He was with the pirates in spring training in 1923.

125
00:13:02,031 --> 00:13:07,273
[SPEAKER_06]: And it looked like he might be able to start at shortstop that year.

126
00:13:08,553 --> 00:13:13,755
[SPEAKER_06]: Whether it was McKechnie who was, he'd be beating out or not, I've forgotten.

127
00:13:13,955 --> 00:13:16,135
[SPEAKER_06]: I didn't really pay too much attention.

128
00:13:16,156 --> 00:13:18,536
[SPEAKER_06]: I was so disappointed not getting interviewed at this point.

129
00:13:21,428 --> 00:13:31,310
[SPEAKER_06]: He was rooming with Ky-Ky Kyler there in training at Pittsburgh, and word came that Kyler was going to be shipped to Nashville for more season.

130
00:13:34,171 --> 00:13:36,191
[SPEAKER_06]: And Michaels wanted to go with Kyler.

131
00:13:36,271 --> 00:13:39,812
[SPEAKER_06]: He liked him, and he thought maybe he wasn't quite ready for the big time.

132
00:13:39,832 --> 00:13:42,153
[SPEAKER_06]: He was coming up too fast, so he liked Kyler.

133
00:13:44,337 --> 00:13:46,719
[SPEAKER_06]: talking around how he would like to go to Nashville.

134
00:13:46,739 --> 00:14:03,492
[SPEAKER_06]: And McKechnie somehow, and I'm not clear what happened to him, McKechnie said something about, well, he would help arrange for his going to Nashville if he wanted to, and could get him a lot more money with Nashville than he had been contracted for with Pittsburgh.

135
00:14:06,674 --> 00:14:07,855
[SPEAKER_06]: And this is the way it worked out.

136
00:14:07,875 --> 00:14:09,156
[SPEAKER_06]: He got twice as much.

137
00:14:09,196 --> 00:14:10,677
[SPEAKER_06]: I don't know the exact amounts now.

138
00:14:11,570 --> 00:14:14,111
[SPEAKER_06]: It must sound like a pretty good pay at Nashville.

139
00:14:15,491 --> 00:14:19,653
[SPEAKER_06]: And so he spent the 1923 season in Nashville.

140
00:14:19,673 --> 00:14:21,493
[SPEAKER_06]: I haven't checked these up in the guides.

141
00:14:21,513 --> 00:14:21,953
[SPEAKER_06]: I don't know.

142
00:14:23,814 --> 00:14:32,657
[SPEAKER_06]: And he said also, I think I got McKechnie was on a spot here because I was rumored I was starting his place there.

143
00:14:33,397 --> 00:14:40,179
[SPEAKER_06]: He wanted to, whether McKechnie was managing the team then, I don't know.

144
00:14:41,320 --> 00:14:50,369
[SPEAKER_06]: This is all kind of confusing, but he said something about getting McKechnie off the spot by his going to Nashville and easing the situation down there in Pitchford.

145
00:14:51,590 --> 00:15:01,080
[SPEAKER_06]: Well, after the 23 season, and I don't know what he did there, he came back up, and this was the year he gave Kaikai Cotter his name.

146
00:15:02,001 --> 00:15:03,102
[SPEAKER_06]: He told me that on the phone.

147
00:15:04,520 --> 00:15:11,521
[SPEAKER_06]: Kyler was playing center field for Nashville, and Michaels was shortstop, and the five balls were one direct hit to the outfield.

148
00:15:11,541 --> 00:15:14,862
[SPEAKER_06]: He would have a short center relay to throw, and he'd yell, Ky, Ky.

149
00:15:14,882 --> 00:15:20,523
[SPEAKER_06]: He'd throw it for Kyler, and they heard it up in the press box, I guess.

150
00:15:25,104 --> 00:15:28,765
[SPEAKER_06]: One of the reporters, Horn, I think his name was,

151
00:15:30,255 --> 00:15:32,737
[SPEAKER_06]: I asked him, what are you calling Kyler out there?

152
00:15:32,777 --> 00:15:35,539
[SPEAKER_06]: He said, I'm not calling him, I'm just yelling Ky, Ky.

153
00:15:36,479 --> 00:15:39,501
[SPEAKER_06]: I said, well that's what it's going to be from now on, he's Ky, Ky, Kyler.

154
00:15:40,742 --> 00:15:42,103
[SPEAKER_06]: That's the story he told me about.

155
00:15:42,143 --> 00:15:45,805
[SPEAKER_06]: Kyler never threw the ball into him, he always threw it way over his head into the home plate.

156
00:15:45,825 --> 00:15:49,388
[SPEAKER_06]: He had such a good arm I guess, but he was out there yelling at him anyhow.

157
00:15:50,388 --> 00:15:52,490
[SPEAKER_06]: That supposedly is how Kyler got his nickname.

158
00:15:56,072 --> 00:15:57,173
[SPEAKER_06]: They came back up to

159
00:15:57,758 --> 00:16:13,862
[SPEAKER_06]: Pittsburgh, either the end of the year or during the winter, and Dreyfus, the Pirate Manor owner, offered Michaels for the 24th season the same salary he'd offered him for 1923, which would be a cut from what he got at Nashville.

160
00:16:15,403 --> 00:16:21,785
[SPEAKER_06]: And he became quite uncooperative, as Michaels did.

161
00:16:22,505 --> 00:16:24,545
[SPEAKER_06]: As he said himself, he's quite chesty.

162
00:16:24,845 --> 00:16:25,446
[SPEAKER_06]: He sure was.

163
00:16:25,486 --> 00:16:26,686
[SPEAKER_06]: He's a little belligerent there.

164
00:16:27,687 --> 00:16:34,032
[SPEAKER_06]: And he said to Dreyfus, why should I take this cut in pay from what I've been getting down at Nashville?

165
00:16:37,755 --> 00:16:42,519
[SPEAKER_06]: And maybe I should be traded or something, or something like that.

166
00:16:42,539 --> 00:16:44,720
[SPEAKER_06]: He was getting a little uppity.

167
00:16:45,541 --> 00:16:55,829
[SPEAKER_06]: And Dreyfus said, Michael said, Dreyfus said, in his Jewish way, he said, well, maybe you speak too soon, something like that.

168
00:16:56,469 --> 00:17:01,534
[SPEAKER_06]: that he was getting a little too impetuous about what he wanted and what he was going to do.

169
00:17:02,154 --> 00:17:07,479
[SPEAKER_06]: He should be more patient about higher pay.

170
00:17:09,701 --> 00:17:13,185
[SPEAKER_06]: So it's a little unclear.

171
00:17:13,205 --> 00:17:20,892
[SPEAKER_06]: Somebody was outside the room, I heard this, and there was a scout for some other club,

172
00:17:23,537 --> 00:17:25,738
[SPEAKER_06]: He said, well, do you want to go someplace else?

173
00:17:25,758 --> 00:17:27,419
[SPEAKER_06]: I said, Michael, yeah, I'd like to.

174
00:17:29,360 --> 00:17:42,267
[SPEAKER_06]: At any rate, he went to Wichita Falls, he was shipped to Wichita Falls in the Texas League for some training, whether this was meaning the Pirates weren't going to use him or what, it isn't clear, but they were playing the Chicago Cubs coming back from Catalina.

