[SPEAKER_06]: I said, well, who doesn't?

184
00:18:22,652 --> 00:18:23,953
[SPEAKER_06]: He said, well, pack your bags.

185
00:18:24,013 --> 00:18:24,773
[SPEAKER_06]: You've been sold.

186
00:18:24,793 --> 00:18:25,853
[SPEAKER_06]: You've been traded to the Cubs.

187
00:18:27,514 --> 00:18:30,235
[SPEAKER_06]: And he said, you know who carried my bags to the station that night?

188
00:18:31,415 --> 00:18:33,355
[SPEAKER_06]: Grover Cleveland Alexander.

189
00:18:35,696 --> 00:18:41,158
[SPEAKER_06]: What on earth Grover Cleveland Alexander was doing carrying Ralph Michaels' bags to the railroad station, I'll never know.

190
00:18:41,938 --> 00:18:42,918
[SPEAKER_06]: And I didn't find out.

191
00:18:44,705 --> 00:18:51,609
[SPEAKER_06]: He kept referring to himself as a young punk, an immature young ball player, a little too chesty maybe.

192
00:18:52,189 --> 00:18:56,931
[SPEAKER_06]: He had a realistic sense about his own value, I guess.

193
00:18:57,952 --> 00:18:59,853
[SPEAKER_06]: But he was trainer of the Cubs for three players.

194
00:18:59,893 --> 00:19:02,414
[SPEAKER_06]: The one I remember is Marty Callahan, an outfielder.

195
00:19:02,774 --> 00:19:03,975
[SPEAKER_06]: But there's an infielder and a pitcher.

196
00:19:05,436 --> 00:19:08,257
[SPEAKER_06]: And so that's how he went to the Cubs for the 1924 season.

197
00:19:09,918 --> 00:19:11,839
[SPEAKER_06]: And the manager of the Cubs in those years was

